I pointed Opus 5.5 (medium reasoning) at the paper, and after a bit of mucking around, instructed it to port the Astra weights for the breakout policy to JavaScript logic. According to its own evaluation, it succeeded. Then I got it to minify the JavaScript policy. The final result is 346B, compared to around 3KB for the (sparse) weights generated by Astra.<p><pre><code>    function policy(x){for(var i=100,p,b,t,s,c,r,a,h,M=Math.tanh,R=v=&gt;v&lt;0?-1-v:v&gt;9?19-v:v;i--;)x[i*6]&amp;&amp;(p=i%10),x[i*6+3]&amp;&amp;(b=i),x[i*6+4]&amp;&amp;(t=i);r=b&#x2F;10|0;c=b%10;s=c&gt;t%10||c==t%10&amp;&amp;c&lt;9?1:-1;a=R(c+s*(8-r));t=t&#x2F;10|0;h=1e3*M(.02*((r&gt;t||r==t&amp;&amp;r-8?r&lt;6?a:(a+R(c+s*(9-r)))&#x2F;2:R(c+s*(r+9-2*(r&lt;2?0:r&lt;4?r:4))))-p));return[0,30*M(-h-15),-100,30\*M(h-15),-100,-100]}
</code></pre>
